**Definition 6.3 (rejection and raw return).** Use ARC's first-detecting
reason tags: malformed0000, descriptor0001, unavailable dependency0010,
no occurrence0011, invalid legacy phase0100, no cover0101, no package0110,
description0111, binding/range1000. A general rejection's raw-request field
is the fixed pair $(\mu,w)$; a mode-zero rejection keeps its original raw
frame field and interpretation. The retained mode tells the client which
grammar to check. All complete general failures in §§3–5 take these
finite paid paths.

From ClientCover/ClientIssue failure first return its invocation TOK to
the handler; their preceding ACK has already freed the mailbox. A handler
failure already holds that slot. Publish Rejection, print, TOK to the
ClientImage rejection branch, import/check retained reason and actual
request, then its usual ACK/mailbox/M/invocation/Ready return. An unexpected
ClientImage binding or codec fault uses ARC's separate finite refusal:
append a tagged fault record on71, ACK/return this busy channel, append
the failed cycle and return Ready. It does not set up a second busy
mailbox or claim success. Authentic channel equality makes that branch
unreachable on a successful request. No rejection path grants a busy
second entry, prematurely reads an unsealed interval, or turns a raw
unterminated supply into a completed request.

